The global spread of the 2019 COVID-19 pandemic caused by SARS-CoV-2 has resulted in widespread losses and socio-economic disruption, with the rapid proliferation of the virus having far-reaching effects worldwide. As part of the effort to combat this crisis, contact tracing technology, known for its success in other outbreaks, has been widely adopted.  However, this technology poses significant concerns regarding user data security, as it entails data collection and analysis that can potentially reveal an individual's identity, location, and behavior. The implications of this are especially dire when considering large-scale government surveillance in countries with restrictive policies.  Despite national laws protecting user data, questions still persist regarding the safeguarding of individual privacy in contact tracing systems.  This study assesses the inherent privacy features of contact tracing COVID-19 apps with specific emphasis on Brazil's national Coronav′irus-SUS app due to Brazil's severe COVID-19 impact.  Additionally, this paper outlines future research directions on this topic in the context of the COVID-19 pandemic. \par

Please note that our primary goal in this work is to offer a constructive analysis, as opposed to a critical one, and to this end we provide a series of measurements to evaluate the potential privacy preservation of contact tracing COVID-19 applications. \end{abstract}

In this paper, we evaluated the inherent privacy features of contact tracing COVID-19 apps with specific emphasis on Brazil's national Coronav′irus-SUS app \cite{breve}. As of February 2021, Coronav′irus-SUS was the only national COVID-19 contact tracing app in use in Brazil and was also adopted by two major healthcare institutions in Brazil as well as other Brazilian state governments. Furthermore, this app was selected for case study due to Brazil's severe COVID-19 impact \cite{covid19br}. Moreover, this app was the first to leverage the Brazilian Healthcare System in contact tracing and was the subject of several prior work regarding user data security \cite{corona2020, corona2021}. Although national laws protecting user data were enacted in Brazil, questions still persist regarding the safeguarding of individual privacy in contact tracing systems. The Coronav′irus-SUS app was the target of our analysis due to its integration with the Healthcare System, as well as its hybrid implementation that concomitantly relies on proximity detection, exposure assessment and medical diagnosis. The app's exposure assessment was based on Brazil's Wi-Fi network through a proprietary technology called geolocalização de exposição de usuários

